WEBVTT

00:00.198 --> 00:02.385
Welcome to this explainer
tailored specifically

00:02.385 --> 00:04.262
for all you educators out there.

00:04.262 --> 00:07.778
Today we're diving straight into
a super practical methodology

00:07.778 --> 00:11.462
from MyMusicBox known as the
3 Weeks of Concept Training.

00:11.462 --> 00:13.742
If you're a teacher
looking for a rock-solid,

00:13.742 --> 00:16.833
reliable blueprint to introduce
and actually solidify musical

00:16.833 --> 00:20.806
concepts in your classroom, well, you
are definitely in the right place.

00:20.806 --> 00:24.099
Let's break down exactly how this
system takes those complex learning

00:24.099 --> 00:26.914
moments and turns them into
highly manageable weekly steps.

00:28.006 --> 00:29.894
Here's our roadmap for today.

00:29.894 --> 00:32.742
First, we'll cover the
five musical foundations.

00:32.742 --> 00:35.462
Second, the concept training timeline.

00:35.462 --> 00:37.894
Third, week one, the introduction.

00:37.894 --> 00:40.194
Fourth, week two, one step more.

00:40.934 --> 00:44.006
Fifth, week three, game
review. And finally,

00:44.006 --> 00:45.538
Concept examples in practice.

00:46.790 --> 00:50.434
Let's jump right into part one,
the five musical foundations.

00:51.206 --> 00:53.915
When you look at the complete
three week cycle framework,

00:53.915 --> 00:55.912
it can seem like a lot, but for right now,

00:55.912 --> 00:58.566
I just want you to focus
purely on the top row.

00:58.566 --> 01:01.958
That row details the five
distinct musical foundations.

01:01.958 --> 01:05.415
And think of these as your ultimate
toolkit, the specific tools you're going

01:05.415 --> 01:08.418
to choose from each week to perfectly
fit the needs of your class.

01:09.350 --> 01:11.681
To make sure we're completely
clear on the facts here,

01:11.681 --> 01:14.022
let's define these five
pillars real quick.

01:14.022 --> 01:15.430
First up is listening.

01:15.430 --> 01:19.718
This is all about training the ear to
compare and identify musical sounds.

01:19.718 --> 01:23.139
Next is movement, which gives
students that essential off-the-piano

01:23.139 --> 01:25.794
coordination to prep both
their body and their mind.

01:26.470 --> 01:29.742
Third is singing, an absolutely
crucial step for internalizing

01:29.742 --> 01:31.906
pitch and melody right through the voice.

01:32.678 --> 01:35.355
Fourth is playing, which is
exactly what it sounds like.

01:35.355 --> 01:38.182
Applying these skills
directly at the keyboard.

01:38.182 --> 01:40.733
And finally, reading, where
we introduce step-by-step

01:40.733 --> 01:42.662
note and sight reading practice.

01:42.662 --> 01:44.748
Every single lesson you
teach is going to draw

01:44.748 --> 01:46.562
on one or more of these specific skills.

01:47.462 --> 01:48.674
The concept training timeline.

01:49.670 --> 01:52.461
You know those colored rings you'll
often see in the top left corner

01:52.461 --> 01:55.494
of your training materials? Well,
they aren't just for decoration.

01:55.494 --> 01:58.630
They act as a super quick visual
guide for you, the teacher.

01:58.630 --> 02:01.670
At a mere glance, you can track
exactly where your class is at.

02:01.670 --> 02:04.362
Whether that's week one, two, three, or

02:04.362 --> 02:07.814
the ongoing 3+ phase
for any given concept.

02:07.814 --> 02:11.074
It is a fantastic way to keep your
lesson planning totally organized.

02:12.454 --> 02:13.286
Part 3:

02:13.286 --> 02:15.138
Week 1, the introduction.

02:16.358 --> 02:17.990
What's the game plan for week one?

02:17.990 --> 02:21.094
Your actionable steps here are
incredibly straightforward.

02:21.094 --> 02:23.732
First, simply have the students
watch and listen to the

02:23.732 --> 02:26.274
concept video. The ears
absolutely lead the way here.

02:26.886 --> 02:28.939
Second, you follow that
up with echo singing,

02:28.939 --> 02:31.586
just short little parts where
they repeat right after you.

02:32.198 --> 02:36.070
Finally, you have them meet the core
idea of the concept face to face.

02:36.070 --> 02:39.340
That might be something as simple
as introducing finger numbers,

02:39.340 --> 02:41.537
or maybe just looking
at the five lines and

02:41.537 --> 02:44.486
four spaces of a staff
for the very first time.

02:44.486 --> 02:48.354
The goal? A gentle, clear introduction
so nobody feels overwhelmed.

02:49.318 --> 02:50.182
Part 4: Week 2.

02:50.182 --> 02:51.234
One step more.

02:52.038 --> 02:55.394
Week two is literally all
about taking one step more.

02:56.198 --> 02:59.104
In just a super short visit of
a few minutes during your class,

02:59.104 --> 03:02.150
you're going to have the kids
listen to the material again.

03:02.150 --> 03:05.094
Then, simply ask them to recall
what they learned last week.

03:05.094 --> 03:08.934
Once they've reestablished that baseline,
you build on it with something brand new.

03:08.934 --> 03:13.164
So if they learned "Do, Re, Mi" in week
one, week two is where you might add "Fa,

03:13.164 --> 03:17.542
Sol, La, Ti, Do." Or maybe you just
tackle the next section of a longer song.

03:17.542 --> 03:20.610
It's all about that incremental,
totally manageable progress.

03:21.382 --> 03:22.342
Part 5: Week 3.

03:22.342 --> 03:23.074
Game review.

03:23.910 --> 03:28.277
By week three, we are bringing the
energy. This week is all about review,

03:28.277 --> 03:30.490
but we're talking an active, engaging

03:30.490 --> 03:33.606
review using movement,
singing, and reading.

03:33.606 --> 03:36.390
The students are going to sing the
concept from memory or maybe from

03:36.390 --> 03:39.366
their book page. Then you review
the whole thing through a game.

03:39.366 --> 03:42.361
This could be clapping hands,
singing the sequence backward,

03:42.361 --> 03:44.958
pointing to lines and spaces,
or even drawing notes.

03:44.958 --> 03:48.003
You're getting their bodies and
their eyes actively involved,

03:48.003 --> 03:50.150
which gives the concept
this great physical

03:50.150 --> 03:52.546
dimension that really
locks in long-term memory.

03:53.510 --> 03:55.558
And finally, part six.

03:55.558 --> 03:57.282
Concept examples in practice.

03:58.246 --> 04:01.258
Let's actually look at how this
plays out in the real world.

04:01.258 --> 04:04.922
If you map out the progression of
three different concepts from book one,

04:04.922 --> 04:07.632
let's say the Finger Song,
the Do-Re-Mi scale, and the

04:07.632 --> 04:10.982
Song of the Music Staff, you
can clearly track the journey.

04:10.982 --> 04:14.252
Notice how you can tag each week
to show exactly which of those five

04:14.252 --> 04:17.378
musical foundations you're using
at every single step of the way.

04:18.630 --> 04:21.606
Let's isolate the Finger Song
just to see the contrast.

04:21.606 --> 04:25.638
In week one, you're focusing entirely
on listening, movement, and singing.

04:25.638 --> 04:27.683
You're introducing finger
numbers one through

04:27.683 --> 04:29.410
five using fun characters and a chant.

04:30.086 --> 04:33.196
But fast forward to week three,
and that exact same concept

04:33.196 --> 04:36.198
has morphed into a physical
clap-your-hands game.

04:36.198 --> 04:38.839
The kids are mastering the
exact same core knowledge,

04:38.839 --> 04:42.626
but the angle of approach has completely
shifted, so it stays fresh and fun.

04:43.430 --> 04:46.342
It's the same story with
the Song of the Music Staff.

04:46.342 --> 04:48.550
Week one is just about
the basic geography,

04:48.550 --> 04:52.422
right? The five lines and four
spaces using listening and reading.

04:52.422 --> 04:55.852
Then in week two, you take that
one step more by simply asking

04:55.852 --> 04:58.562
them, "Hey, is this a line
note or a space note?"

04:59.206 --> 05:01.949
The absolute key takeaway here
is that you're not dumping

05:01.949 --> 05:04.422
the entire grand staff on them on day one.

05:04.422 --> 05:06.364
You introduce the staff,
and then next week,

05:06.364 --> 05:08.482
you teach them how to
actually interact with it.

05:09.926 --> 05:12.249
As you take this methodology
into your own classroom,

05:12.249 --> 05:14.178
keep these golden rules
in your back pocket.

05:14.918 --> 05:16.806
Number one, video first.

05:16.806 --> 05:20.194
Every concept has to start with its
video so the ears can lead the way.

05:20.838 --> 05:23.078
Number two, short and often.

05:23.078 --> 05:26.950
Concept work is literally just a
few minutes inside a larger lesson.

05:26.950 --> 05:29.414
Number three, use games for review.

05:29.414 --> 05:31.442
Remember, week three
is your chance to bring

05:31.442 --> 05:33.378
the concept back from a totally new angle.

05:33.990 --> 05:37.438
And lastly, number four, the
learning doesn't magically stop at

05:37.438 --> 05:41.161
week three. In the 3+ phase, these
concepts are going to continually

05:41.161 --> 05:44.226
return as ring games or echo
exercises in future pieces.

05:44.966 --> 05:48.380
Ultimately, this whole structure is
built to give you and your students

05:48.380 --> 05:51.362
a predictable, highly effective
path to true musical literacy.

05:51.974 --> 05:54.383
By cycling through
listening, movement, singing,

05:54.383 --> 05:58.498
playing, and reading, you're ensuring deep
comprehension without burning them out.

05:59.462 --> 06:02.100
As you sit down to look at
your upcoming lesson plan,

06:02.100 --> 06:04.937
ask yourself, how are you
gonna transform a complex piece

06:04.937 --> 06:08.326
of musical theory into a simple,
joyful game by week three?

06:08.326 --> 06:10.615
Because the foundation you
choose today is exactly

06:10.615 --> 06:12.354
what builds the musicians of tomorrow.
